---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="11-debugging"
  lessonSlug="03-print-vs-pdb"
  hook="Two semantic-error functions. Print debugging finds both. Brief intro to pdb for the day prints stop being enough."
>
  <h2>Bug 1 — forgot to divide</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    code={`def average(nums):
    return sum(nums)             # ← BUG: never divides

print(average([10, 20, 30]))     # 60 — should be 20.0`}
  />

  <h2>Bug 2 — boundary check</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    code={`def in_range(value, low, high):
    return low < value < high    # ← BUG: < excludes boundaries

print(in_range(1, 1, 10))        # False — should be True (inclusive)`}
  />

  <h2>The fixes</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def average(nums):
    if not nums:
        return 0.0               # guard against ZeroDivisionError
    return sum(nums) / len(nums)

def in_range(value, low, high):
    return low <= value <= high

print(average([10, 20, 30]))    # 20.0
print(average([]))               # 0.0
print(in_range(1, 1, 10))       # True
print(in_range(10, 1, 10))      # True
print(in_range(0, 1, 10))       # False`}
  />

  <Callout tone="tip" title="Chained comparison">
    <p>
      <code>low &lt;= value &lt;= high</code> is exactly the math
      <em>low ≤ value ≤ high</em>. Python's chained comparison reads
      naturally — use it.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>When print stops being enough — try pdb</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    caption="In a real script you'd add breakpoint() and run with python (not in browser)"
    code={`# In your terminal — Python 3.7+:
#
#     def average(nums):
#         breakpoint()                 # ← drops into (Pdb) prompt here
#         return sum(nums) / len(nums)
#
# At the (Pdb) prompt:
#   p nums           # print the value of nums
#   p sum(nums)      # try any expression
#   n                # next line
#   c                # continue
#   q                # quit
#
# For an entry-level course, print covers 90% of bugs.
# pdb is the tool for when print produces too much noise.

print("(pdb is interactive — try it in your terminal, not the browser)")`}
  />

  <Callout tone="note" title="From Think Python App.A">
    <p>
      <em>"One of the problems with using print statements for debugging
      is that you can end up buried in output. There are two ways to
      proceed: simplify the output or simplify the program."</em>
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Lecture>
